[ENS DAO](https://ensdao.org/) is the decentralized autonomous organization governing the protocol Ethereum Name Service (ENS). [ENS](https://ens.domains/) is a protocol on Ethereum that maps human-readable domain names (e.g., hex6c.eth) to blockchain addresses and other metadata. 

In early 2022 the DAO of ENS submitted on [Snapshot](https://snapshot.org) (an off-chain voting platform widely used by DAOs) the [proposal](https://snapshot.org/#/s:ens.eth/proposal/0xa9a2dc5a52ea54b478c4c5fea88540622dff7ad5000f8d146dff482c6e6b6055) "*Removal of Brantly Millegan as Director of The ENS Foundation*". The proposal seeks to remove Brantly Millegan as Director of The ENS Foundation due to his publicly held controversial views on social issues that are viewed as hateful and discriminatory. 


## Collect data

Let's collect the voting data of the proposal using [GraphQL](https://graphql.org/). 

GraphQL (Graph Query Language) is a language for querying and manipulating data through APIs. It was developed by Facebook in 2012 and released as an open-source project in 2015.

1. A GraphQL API exposes a schema describing the available data types, their fields, and the relationships between them.
2. A client sends a query to a GraphQL server, which processes the request and returns the requested data, typically in JSON format.

The following query retrieves the votes casted by the voters:

```
query Votes {
  votes(
    where: {
      proposal: "0xa9a2dc5a52ea54b478c4c5fea88540622dff7ad5000f8d146dff482c6e6b6055"
    }
    first: 1000
    orderBy: "vp"
    orderDirection: desc
  ) {
    voter
    choice
    vp
  }
}
```

1. Run the query on the [Snapshot GraphQL API](https://hub.snapshot.org/graphql) ([API documentation](https://docs.snapshot.box/tools/api))  
2. Save the result in a JSON file called `voting.json`.

```{r eval=FALSE}
library(tidyverse)
library(jsonlite)

# get a tibble from JSON file
json <- fromJSON("voting.json", flatten = TRUE)
votes = as_tibble(json$data$votes)

# save as CSV
write_csv(votes, file = "votes.csv")
```

Or use GraphQL programmatically:

```{r eval=FALSE}
library(tidyverse)
library(httr2)

# Define the GraphQL query
query <- '
query Votes {
  votes(
    where: {
      proposal: "0xa9a2dc5a52ea54b478c4c5fea88540622dff7ad5000f8d146dff482c6e6b6055"
    }
    first: 1000
    orderBy: "vp"
    orderDirection: desc
  ) {
    voter
    choice
    vp
  }
}
'

# Send the query
response <- 
  request("https://hub.snapshot.org/graphql") |>
  req_body_json(
    list(query = query)
  ) |>
  req_perform() |>
  resp_body_json(
    simplifyVector = TRUE
  )

# Get the votes
votes = as_tibble(response$data$votes)

# Save as CSV
write_csv(votes, "votes.csv")

```



## Concentration of voting power

Let's investigate the concentration of voting power for both choices Yes and No using Gini index and Lorenz curve. 


```{r}
library(ineq)
library(tidyverse)

votes = read_csv("votes.csv")

yes = 
  votes %>% 
  filter(choice == 1) %>% 
  pull(vp)

no = 
  votes %>% 
  filter(choice == 2) %>% 
  pull(vp)

# Gini index
(gini_yes = Gini(yes))
(gini_no = Gini(no))

# Lorenz curve
Lc1 <- Lc(yes)
Lc2 <- Lc(no)

df_lorenz <- rbind(
  data.frame(p = Lc1$p, L = Lc1$L, group = "Yes"),
  data.frame(p = Lc2$p, L = Lc2$L, group = "No")
)
```


```{r}
ggplot(df_lorenz, aes(p, L, colour = group)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1) +
  geom_abline(slope = 1, intercept = 0, linetype = "dashed") +
  labs(
    title = "Lorenz curve for approval (Yes) and denial (No) options",
    subtitle = "Both voting distributions are highly concentrated, with Gini above 0.9",
    x = "Cumulative population share",
    y = "Cumulative value share",
    colour = ""
  ) +
  theme_minimal()

```

## Voting strategies

Let's check the outcomes using one-person-one-vote, one-token-one-vote, as well as Quadratic Voting strategies.

```{r}
outcomes = 
  votes |> 
  group_by(choice) |>
  summarise(one_person_one_vote = n(), one_token_one_vote = sum(vp), qv = sum(sqrt(vp))) |> 
  mutate(share_one_person_one_vote = one_person_one_vote / sum(one_person_one_vote),
         share_one_token_one_vote = one_token_one_vote / sum(one_token_one_vote),
         share_qv = qv / sum(qv))

outcomes |> 
  filter(choice %in% c(1, 2)) |> 
  select(choice, contains("share")) |> 
  round(3)


```


